From 94bfd8267dc2d0398d1b570672f8cfba2250a9c9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tryinget <markus@team-wangler.de>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 00:11:16 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 04/13] feat(cli): surf extract runs a read-only script in an
 owned tab

`surf extract <url> --file rows.js` composes existing tools into the
extraction lifecycle agents keep re-deriving by hand:

  tab.new -> wait.ready -> js -> parse -> rows invariant -> tab.close

- Owned tab: the command opens its own tab and always closes it, also
  when readiness or the script fails, so no tab leaks.
- Readiness gate: --ready-selector/--ready-text/--ready-url-prefix/
  --empty-text go to wait.ready; a login bounce, challenge, not-found
  or error page fails the run with that typed code and is never
  retried, since the next tab would land on the same page.
- Bounded fresh-tab retry (--retry, default 1, max 5): transient
  target failures ("navigated or closed", detached target, lost
  execution context, unreachable content script), readiness timeouts
  and zero-row results close the failed tab and start over in a new
  one. A tab whose context was invalidated is never reused.
- Zero rows is a failure unless --allow-empty is passed or the page
  showed its own no-results message (--empty-text). This separates
  "empty" from "logged out, blocked, or selectors missed".
- Read-only by contract: the whole sequence may replay, so a script
  that mutates state must run through `surf js` on an explicit target.
  With --tab-id/--session the command reads that tab in place,
  navigates only when a URL is given, and never retries or closes it.
- Options prelude: --options '{"limit": 20}' is exposed to the script
  as a frozen SURF_OPTIONS constant (native/script-options.cjs), so
  the script stays a plain file usable in other page-JS tools.
- Output: Markdown (metadata bullets plus a row table) by default,
  --json for {data, rows, rowCount, attempts, readiness}.

native/extract.cjs holds the pure pieces (error classifier, row
selection, invariant, Markdown renderer) and runExtraction, which
takes an injected executeTool; the tests script a fake host and assert
the exact call sequence, including the close-then-reopen retry.

